            <ArticleTitle>A simple model of the hemodynamic effects of a proximal coronary artery narrowing.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>309-12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A simple mathematical model of the hemodynamic effects of a discrete proximal coronary artery obstruction is presented. The model demonstrates that resting myocardial flow demands fail to be met when the vessel diameter is decreased by 75% or greater. More strikingly, it shows that the maximum flow obtainable for increased myocardial oxygen demand begins to fall significantly with a lesion causing a 40 to 50% obstruction. The implications of these findings in relation to stress-induced angina are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
